WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:10.120
- Good morning, everyone.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:13.130
I speak for the entire university

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:15.830
when I say that it is a deep honor

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:20.560
and a very sincere pleasure to
welcome Daw Aung San Suu Kyi,

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.050
chair of the National League
for Democracy in Burma,

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:27.620
a member of parliament to Yale.

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:29.990
It is just a very special privilege for us

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:31.930
to have you with us.

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:33.960
I believe that everyone in this audience

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:38.960
knows the basic outline of
Daw Suu's extraordinary story.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.390
The daughter of Aung
San who liberated Burma

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:45.670
from colonial rule before being cruelly

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:48.510
assassinated in 1947,

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:51.770
she spent many years outside
Burma being educated at Oxford

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:53.890
and raising a family there.

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:55.840
When her mother became very ill,

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:59.400
she returned to Burma in 1988

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.680
only to find that a student
uprising was restoring

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:05.650
some sense of hope to
the millions of Burmese

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:08.290
under oppressive military rule.

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:12.360
She quickly became the leader
of the movement for democracy

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:17.230
assuming the mantle with
humility, bravery and courage.

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:20.010
She famously defied
soldiers whose loaded guns

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:25.010
were aimed directly at her
inspiring the U2 song, Walk On.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.270
She was placed under house
arrest soon thereafter

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:33.963
and spent more than 15 of the
next 20 years in isolation.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:38.850
Released from house
arrest in November 2010

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:42.210
and elected to parliament
in April of this year,

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:45.060
she has continued her
crusade for the rights

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.870
of the Burmese people.

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:50.030
Thanks to Daw Suu's
courage and the inspiration

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:53.080
she is provided to the people of Burma,

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:56.110
the prospects for the
restoration of democracy

00:01:56.110 --> 00:02:00.000
are greater now than they
have been for decades.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.700
Since her release from house arrest,

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:05.250
Daw Suu has partnered with
Burma's new civilian government

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.500
to help lead a period of rapid political

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:11.000
and economic liberalization.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.860
The government has released
hundreds of political prisoners

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:17.230
and granted substantial
freedom to the press.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:19.130
The United States and many other nations

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:22.210
have reciprocated these
actions by easing sanctions

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:24.500
and pledging foreign aid.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:29.090
But because a democratic
future is not fully assured,

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:31.203
Daw Suu's work is not yet done.

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:35.350
Daw Suu was honored with the
Nobel Peace Prize in 1991

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:38.083
and with a Congressional
Gold Medal last week.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:41.470
Jane and I had the
privilege of hearing her

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:45.640
speak last Friday when she
received the Global Citizen Award

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:47.960
from the Atlantic Council.

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:52.773
As you will see momentarily,
she is a true inspiration.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:57.460
In a beautiful essay that
Daw Suu wrote in 1990

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:00.020
entitled Freedom from Fear,

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:04.910
she claimed that Nehru words
describing Mahatma Gandhi

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:08.433
could be applied to her father, Aung San.

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:12.760
I would suggest that they
apply equally well to her

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:15.500
and I paraphrase here
to get the gender right.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:19.700
The essence of her teaching
is fearlessness and truth

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:21.700
and action allied to these

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:24.663
always keeping the welfare
of the masses in view.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:28.170
As she, herself, has said about the poetry

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:31.570
that she reveres and from
which she takes strength,

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:35.040
Daw Suu is, and I quote, a great unifier

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:38.343
that knows no frontiers of space or time.

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:41.410
Today, I'm pleased to announce that Yale

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:44.290
is taking several small
steps to facilitate

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:46.650
the reforms underway in Burma.

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:50.650
First, we are stepping up our
admissions recruitment efforts

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:52.330
for students from Burma

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:55.460
and we will be communicating more broadly

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:57.320
the fact little known there

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:59.850
that the parents of
applicants to Yale College

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:04.030
who make less than $65,000
a year are not required

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:06.513
to make a contribution
to their child's tuition.

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:10.470
Second, we will work to implement

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:13.840
an idea discussed yesterday with Daw Suu

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:17.130
to bring high school teachers
and principals to Yale

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:20.420
to encourage them to raise
the sights of their students

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:23.610
to advise them, and to
advise them on the generous

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:26.600
financial aid programs available at Yale

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:28.423
and elsewhere in the United States.

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:32.550
And finally, I'm particularly
pleased to announce

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:36.320
that with the cooperation of
the Council On Southeast Asia

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:40.170
Studies at Yale, we will
support the establishment

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:41.870
of a successor publication

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:44.810
to the Journal of the
Burma Research Society,

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:47.960
a prominent vehicle for
scholarly publication on Burma

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:52.663
before it was shut down by the
military regime in the 1970s.

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:57.060
As Dean Brenzel mentioned to you,

00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:00.170
the Chubb Fellowship is
among the highest honors

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:02.860
accorded to a visitor at Yale.

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:05.510
Since 1949, Chubb Fellows have included

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:07.520
former presidents of the United States,

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:11.060
numerous heads of state
and prominent figures

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:13.720
from government, industry and the arts.

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:18.070
Today's Chubb Fellow adds
luster to that luminous list.

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:21.399
Please join me in
welcoming Aung San Suu Kyi.

00:05:21.399 --> 00:05:24.649
(attendees applauding)

00:05:27.893 --> 00:05:32.003
(speaking in foreign language)

00:05:32.003 --> 00:05:35.253
(attendees applauding)

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:50.253
- Thank you, thank you
for a very warm welcome.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:55.540
The subject on which
I'm going to speak today

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:57.350
is rule of law.

00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:01.760
Not rule of law as you might
be taught in your classrooms,

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:04.700
that, your professors can
do far more competently

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:09.430
than I could ever hope to
but rule of law as I see it

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:12.300
and others like me in Burma see it

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:15.363
and why rule of law is important to us.

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:22.190
To begin with, I had a very healthy

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:26.603
minimum contact with the law until 1988.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:32.090
Up to 1988, the only two
contacts, shall we say,

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:35.700
I had had with the law
whereas a student at Oxford.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:39.820
Once, I was cycling the wrong
way down a one-way street

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:42.893
and ran straight into a police patrol car.

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:47.080
The policeman put out
his head and looked at me

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:50.480
and asked me if I knew
that was a one-way street.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:54.090
I said, yes, so he proceeded on his way

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:57.240
and I hopped off my bike
and proceeded on mine.

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:00.760
The second time also was at Oxford when,

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:03.990
at an artist before dusk
and the roads were empty.

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:07.240
I cycled absent-mindedly
through a red light

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:11.160
and the roads were empty
but for a patrol car

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:13.400
which is right behind me.

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:16.350
So I should have known
then that my contacts

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:19.723
of the security forces were
going to be unique in future.

00:07:21.070 --> 00:07:25.640
The real contact came in 1988 and '89

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:28.790
when I was first placed
under house arrest.

00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:33.790
This was on the 20th
July, 1989 and suddenly,

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:38.020
hordes of policemen and uniformed soldiers

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:42.900
came into my compound and
rounded up everybody there.

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:44.510
I was kept in the house.

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:47.550
I thought they were going to
take me away to prison as well

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:50.640
but they left me in the
house in the house arrest

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:52.863
and took the others away to prison.

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:57.260
They had brought a detention
order and they read it up.

00:07:57.260 --> 00:08:01.523
This order said that they were,

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:04.920
they were placing me under house arrest

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:09.420
in accordance with the law meant to,

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:11.320
how did they put it?

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:16.320
To protect the state against
destructive elements.

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:19.540
I was a destructive element so this meant

00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:23.110
that I could be placed under
house arrest without trial.

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:25.330
Now this is section 10.

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:30.330
Section 10 has two parts
to it, 10A and 10B.

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:33.750
10A means imprisonment without trial.

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:37.080
10B is house arrest without trial.

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:40.540
I was placed at a house arrest and a 10B.

00:08:40.540 --> 00:08:44.120
According to that law, the detention order

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:46.780
would have to be renewed every six months

00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:51.590
and was renewable for a
total period of three years

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:54.760
and the decision as to whether or not

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:56.430
they would renew the detention order

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:58.280
would be made by what they call

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:03.200
the central committee of ministers.

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:05.510
This is a small group of
three or four ministers

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:07.223
including the home minister.

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:12.330
At that time, I had had
so very little to do

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:15.430
with such matters that I had assumed

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:18.350
that they had placed me under house arrest

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:21.103
because this was in accordance with law.

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:23.860
I was alone in the house.

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:26.040
My two sons were with me for a short while

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:29.200
but then they left to go back to England

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:33.490
but they kept me in the
house and the security forces

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:37.300
took up their residence in my compound.

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:38.850
I thought also then

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:41.410
that this was what they
were entitled to do.

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:43.520
It was only years later I learned

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:47.000
that they were not entitled
to take up residence

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:49.300
within my compound.

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:51.910
So this is the beginning of the time

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:54.300
when I realized how very little

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:56.840
I knew about the laws of the land

00:09:56.840 --> 00:09:59.910
and how very little I
knew about whether or not

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:01.633
these laws were respected.

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:05.310
I was allowed a shortwave radio

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:07.730
which get me in touch with outside world

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:10.550
and I learned from this
radio that more and more

00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:13.890
of the members of my
party were been arrested

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:18.890
and most often, they were
arrested under section five.

00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:24.597
Section five is the law
that was brought out in 1950

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:30.610
to cope with what they called emergency,

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:33.740
emergency situation of that time

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:36.033
because there were many insurgencies then.

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:41.311
Under section five, we used to,

00:10:41.311 --> 00:10:45.400
later, we, politicians
sort of refer to it as

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:47.900
I can't bear you law.

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:49.230
Anybody that they didn't like,

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:52.020
they could grab under section
five and put them away

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:55.430
for a period of up to
five years but they would

00:10:56.710 --> 00:11:00.240
slap other charges against these people

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:03.220
and could give them up to 20
years which they have done.

00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:04.820
I think our longest

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:08.120
that a member of our party who served

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:10.140
the longest single period in jail

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:13.550
stayed there for 18 years and three months

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:16.230
although he was originally
supposed to have been imprisoned

00:11:16.230 --> 00:11:18.820
only for about five years.

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:23.700
So my own experiences made me understand

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:26.140
that there was no rule of law in Burma.

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:30.980
The law was used by those in
authority for their own ends.

00:11:30.980 --> 00:11:35.330
The law was not there to protect
the people of the country.

00:11:35.330 --> 00:11:39.190
Section five, section 10 and
then there were other sections

00:11:39.190 --> 00:11:41.520
which I became very familiar with.

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:44.750
Section 17 which was
to do with association

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:47.620
in the unlawful organizations.

00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:51.220
These were the sections
used by the military regime

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:53.640
to put the members of the NLD in prison

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:55.810
at any time they felt like it

00:11:55.810 --> 00:11:59.270
and to keep them for
as long as they wished.

00:11:59.270 --> 00:12:04.270
Section 10 was amended when the time came

00:12:04.730 --> 00:12:06.950
near for my release three years

00:12:06.950 --> 00:12:10.010
after I had first been
placed under house arrest.

00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:12.650
They changed it so that
they could keep me in

00:12:12.650 --> 00:12:15.070
for more than that and in fact,

00:12:15.070 --> 00:12:17.063
they kept me in for six years.

00:12:18.040 --> 00:12:20.210
Have you ever heard of a law been changed

00:12:20.210 --> 00:12:24.120
simply to keep one
person longer in prison?

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:27.120
This shows you what it meant

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:29.313
not to be under the rule of law.

00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:33.220
While I was under house arrest

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:35.360
and listening to the news of the arrest

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:38.600
of so many of my colleagues,

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:42.530
I decided that we needed
a legal aid committee.

00:12:42.530 --> 00:12:45.380
We didn't have any such
thing in our country.

00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:47.440
I don't think it had ever been known.

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:49.100
So the moment I was released

00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:51.773
after my first term of
house arrest in 1995,

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:55.690
I explained to our party

00:12:55.690 --> 00:12:58.650
that we must have a legal aid committee.

00:12:58.650 --> 00:13:01.840
The person I chose to head this committee

00:13:01.840 --> 00:13:05.570
was then the chairman of our NLD, U Tin Oo

00:13:05.570 --> 00:13:07.120
who used to be the commander-in-chief

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:11.420
of the Burmese army and
he was a trained lawyer.

00:13:11.420 --> 00:13:15.023
He took up door after he
was expelled from the army.

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:19.740
I asked him to form
this legal aid committee

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:24.538
to protect those of our
colleagues who were charged

00:13:24.538 --> 00:13:26.790
under some section or the other

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:30.400
but all these sections were just an excuse

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:32.310
for putting them away,

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:35.840
for keeping them out of active politics.

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:39.200
And when I put the suggestion
to U Tin Oo, he was not,

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:42.630
he was a very,

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:44.000
well, he still is

00:13:45.345 --> 00:13:47.980
a really good professional soldier.

00:13:47.980 --> 00:13:52.280
He obeys orders in a very
straightforward manner

00:13:52.280 --> 00:13:53.520
and he always looked upon me

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:56.510
as what he used to call his commander.

00:13:56.510 --> 00:13:59.070
So when I said please, would you head

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:00.290
the legal aid committee?

00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:03.410
He said, yes, but months later he told me

00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:05.640
that he didn't believe in it.

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:07.690
He said yes and he took on this job

00:14:07.690 --> 00:14:09.300
because I had asked him to.

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:12.100
He did not believe that
it would do any good

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:15.500
because he said we would
never have won any case

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:17.820
when we were trying to
defend our colleagues

00:14:17.820 --> 00:14:19.510
who had been charged with,

00:14:19.510 --> 00:14:23.390
charged under section five
or 10 or any of those other

00:14:23.390 --> 00:14:25.530
very questionable sections.

00:14:25.530 --> 00:14:28.640
But I explained to him that I knew

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:31.410
we would probably not be
able to win the freedom

00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:34.590
of our colleagues once
they had been arrested

00:14:34.590 --> 00:14:37.240
but first of all, I wanted our people

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:41.270
to be confident that they
would not be abandoned,

00:14:41.270 --> 00:14:44.550
that they would be
defended as far as possible

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:47.370
in accordance with the laws of the land.

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:50.580
And secondly, I said that all these cases

00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:52.400
would remain on record

00:14:52.400 --> 00:14:54.890
and that was necessary for the future.

00:14:54.890 --> 00:14:58.160
These cases of lack of rule of law

00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:01.790
would be there forever on
record for future generations

00:15:01.790 --> 00:15:03.403
to see what they should avoid.

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:07.000
He accepted my explanation

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:10.020
but he showed no indication whatsoever

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:12.333
that he didn't really think it was,

00:15:14.500 --> 00:15:17.140
it would have any positive results

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:19.960
but later, he admitted that he was wrong.

00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:22.780
The heart that we put into our colleagues,

00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:25.820
they all knew that they would
probably not get away with it,

00:15:25.820 --> 00:15:30.040
that we would not be able
to obtain the release

00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:32.590
but they were happy
that somebody was there

00:15:32.590 --> 00:15:35.600
to stand up in court to defend them

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:37.040
which had not been the case

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:39.540
before we found the legal aid committee.

00:15:39.540 --> 00:15:42.760
A few of our brave
lawyers had come forward

00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:44.880
to defend prisoners of conscience

00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:47.780
to those charged under
those various sections

00:15:47.780 --> 00:15:52.610
but we had never won a case
and we didn't win any cases.

00:15:52.610 --> 00:15:55.980
Of the thousands of
cases that were taken up

00:15:55.980 --> 00:15:59.670
by our legal aid
committee, we won only one,

00:15:59.670 --> 00:16:03.650
one single case and the judge was a woman.

00:16:03.650 --> 00:16:05.980
I don't know whether
thus is any significance

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:09.930
but I must also add that after

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:14.730
she had passed judgment
freeing our prisoner.

00:16:14.730 --> 00:16:19.040
she was sent off to a remote
provincial town as a punishment

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:22.090
for daring to do justice by us.

00:16:22.090 --> 00:16:27.090
So we learned as we went along
that without rule of law,

00:16:27.450 --> 00:16:31.280
our people were never
going to be free from fear.

00:16:31.280 --> 00:16:33.120
They would always be afraid

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:35.350
that even if they had done nothing wrong,

00:16:35.350 --> 00:16:38.170
action could be taken against them

00:16:38.170 --> 00:16:41.800
in accordance with the
so-called laws of the land.

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:46.800
So the importance of rule
of law was made known to us

00:16:47.370 --> 00:16:49.063
through our daily experience.

00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:55.410
Many of our people in
those days were arrested

00:16:55.410 --> 00:17:00.020
for such small matters as the distribution

00:17:00.020 --> 00:17:03.110
of the Universal
Declaration of Human Rights.

00:17:03.110 --> 00:17:05.750
This was considered very dangerous.

00:17:05.750 --> 00:17:09.820
The NLD was trying to let our people know

00:17:09.820 --> 00:17:13.730
what their rights were
because Burma was a signatory

00:17:13.730 --> 00:17:16.370
of the Universal
Declaration of Human Rights

00:17:16.370 --> 00:17:19.130
and in fact, at that
time, the military regime

00:17:19.130 --> 00:17:22.630
had come to an agreement
with the United Nations

00:17:22.630 --> 00:17:27.170
to disseminate the Universal
Declaration of Human Rights

00:17:27.170 --> 00:17:29.380
as widely as possible.

00:17:29.380 --> 00:17:32.420
So what we were doing was in accordance

00:17:32.420 --> 00:17:37.000
with what the military
government had undertaken to do

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:41.150
and yet, some of our
people were arrested simply

00:17:41.150 --> 00:17:43.070
for distributing copies

00:17:43.070 --> 00:17:45.880
of the Universal
Declaration of Human Rights.

00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:50.880
So for us human rights and
rule of law went together.

00:17:50.930 --> 00:17:54.430
It says in the preamble to the
United Nations' declaration

00:17:54.430 --> 00:17:58.060
that human rights should be
protected by the rule of law

00:17:58.060 --> 00:18:01.460
and we believe that this
is absolutely essential.

00:18:01.460 --> 00:18:03.000
Unless there's rule of law,

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:06.900
there can be no guarantee of human rights.

00:18:06.900 --> 00:18:08.003
So,

00:18:09.070 --> 00:18:14.070
when the NLD decided to contest
the by-elections last April,

00:18:15.950 --> 00:18:18.120
we put forward as the main plank

00:18:19.282 --> 00:18:21.950
of our election platform, rule of law.

00:18:21.950 --> 00:18:23.850
There are three main planks.

00:18:23.850 --> 00:18:26.630
Rule of law, an end to ethnic conflict

00:18:26.630 --> 00:18:28.743
and amendments to the constitution.

00:18:30.430 --> 00:18:33.270
Some have questioned whether it was right

00:18:33.270 --> 00:18:37.490
to put rule of law before
end to ethnic conflict.

00:18:37.490 --> 00:18:41.400
We did that because we think
that practically speaking,

00:18:41.400 --> 00:18:44.050
we cannot bring an end
to the ethnic conflict

00:18:44.050 --> 00:18:48.440
unless there's rule of
law, unless we can assure

00:18:48.440 --> 00:18:53.250
our ethnic nationalities that
justice will not only be done

00:18:53.250 --> 00:18:55.940
but seen to be done for them.

00:18:55.940 --> 00:19:00.940
They would not be able to
support any peace process

00:19:00.940 --> 00:19:02.710
with full confidence.

00:19:02.710 --> 00:19:06.460
So we think rule of law is the first step

00:19:06.460 --> 00:19:10.110
towards a genuine
democratic society in Burma.

00:19:10.110 --> 00:19:12.980
And because of what we have suffered,

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:17.290
we knew from experience that rule of law

00:19:17.290 --> 00:19:20.690
is what rules our lives from day to day

00:19:20.690 --> 00:19:24.310
and if it is rule of unjust laws,

00:19:24.310 --> 00:19:28.840
then we are ruled
unjustly from day to day.

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:31.240
These sections that I
have mentioned earlier,

00:19:31.240 --> 00:19:36.240
sections 10 and five should
never have been practiced.

00:19:37.965 --> 00:19:41.450
We want these to be removed completely

00:19:41.450 --> 00:19:43.150
from the body of our law

00:19:43.150 --> 00:19:45.250
but this has not yet been possible.

00:19:45.250 --> 00:19:47.703
We are trying to,

00:19:49.187 --> 00:19:52.650
we are trying very hard
within the legislature

00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:54.160
but I have to remind everybody

00:19:54.160 --> 00:19:59.160
that we are very small minority
as yet to remove these laws

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:02.170
which are misused by the authorities

00:20:02.170 --> 00:20:06.530
to deprive the people of the basic rights.

00:20:06.530 --> 00:20:09.713
You can charge anybody
with those sections.

00:20:11.160 --> 00:20:15.470
When you say that somebody
is a destructive element,

00:20:15.470 --> 00:20:17.480
what exactly does it mean?

00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:21.670
How do you prove whether or
not I'm a destructive element?

00:20:21.670 --> 00:20:24.130
But the authorities decided

00:20:24.130 --> 00:20:26.040
that I was a destructive element.

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:29.640
I was a danger to the peace
and tranquility of the state

00:20:29.640 --> 00:20:33.370
and therefore, I could be
put away for years and years.

00:20:33.370 --> 00:20:37.560
The first time was six years
and then about two years

00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:41.110
and then the last time again, six years.

00:20:41.110 --> 00:20:41.943
So,

00:20:44.440 --> 00:20:47.560
I was, of course, much
better off under house arrest

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:50.940
and my colleagues in prison but

00:20:52.170 --> 00:20:56.430
the section under which
they were placed in prison,

00:20:56.430 --> 00:21:00.790
section five is equally destructive

00:21:00.790 --> 00:21:02.720
of basic human rights

00:21:03.910 --> 00:21:06.740
because there is,

00:21:06.740 --> 00:21:11.280
section five has a very peculiar

00:21:11.280 --> 00:21:16.280
part to it that if you act
in such a way as to diminish

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:20.367
the love of the people for the government,

00:21:20.367 --> 00:21:23.370
then you can be charged
under section five.

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:25.980
Now, how do you decide whether or not

00:21:25.980 --> 00:21:27.950
you have done anything to diminish

00:21:27.950 --> 00:21:30.023
somebody's love from something else?

00:21:31.070 --> 00:21:33.990
It's so vague and it's so broad,

00:21:33.990 --> 00:21:36.730
you can just pull anybody in.

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:40.830
This is why we decided that
we would run for by-elections

00:21:40.830 --> 00:21:45.563
on this platform putting
rule of law at the very head.

00:21:47.330 --> 00:21:52.330
And after we contested
by-elections and we did well,

00:21:52.700 --> 00:21:56.970
we won 43 out of the 44
seats that we contested,

00:21:56.970 --> 00:22:00.490
we entered the parliament
at the National Assembly

00:22:00.490 --> 00:22:03.130
and since I've been in
the National Assembly,

00:22:03.130 --> 00:22:04.900
the speaker has made me chairman

00:22:04.900 --> 00:22:08.510
of the Committee for Rule
of Law and Tranquility.

00:22:08.510 --> 00:22:09.450
I know you will laugh.

00:22:09.450 --> 00:22:13.560
Everybody smiles when I say
rule of law and tranquility

00:22:13.560 --> 00:22:15.563
but it's a nice thought.

00:22:16.413 --> 00:22:19.496
(attendees laughing)

00:22:22.101 --> 00:22:25.530
As a practical step
towards re-establishing

00:22:25.530 --> 00:22:27.160
rule of law in the country,

00:22:27.160 --> 00:22:31.810
we organized a seminar with retired judges

00:22:31.810 --> 00:22:35.850
and experienced lawyers to
find out what we could do

00:22:35.850 --> 00:22:38.093
to restore rule of law in Burma.

00:22:38.990 --> 00:22:41.670
I say restore because once upon a time,

00:22:41.670 --> 00:22:44.400
we did have rule of law in Burma

00:22:44.400 --> 00:22:48.200
and some of the old lawyers
spoke the old judges book

00:22:48.200 --> 00:22:50.690
and one of them taught about

00:22:50.690 --> 00:22:52.500
the independence of the judiciary

00:22:52.500 --> 00:22:57.500
which we had enjoyed after we
became an independent nation.

00:22:57.950 --> 00:23:00.020
We were a parliamentary democracy

00:23:00.020 --> 00:23:03.940
and the judiciary exercised
considerable independence.

00:23:03.940 --> 00:23:08.940
I think if you want to read
about the early history

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:11.010
of the judiciary in Burma,

00:23:11.010 --> 00:23:12.700
you'll have to read Dr. Maung Maung

00:23:12.700 --> 00:23:17.700
who was at Yale's Law School
as a Ford Foundation scholar

00:23:17.790 --> 00:23:21.013
for a couple of years before
the first military coup.

00:23:22.180 --> 00:23:26.830
He later became, for briefly,
the president of Burma

00:23:26.830 --> 00:23:31.120
after the uprising 1988
but he did not stayed

00:23:31.120 --> 00:23:32.453
in his post very long.

00:23:33.810 --> 00:23:36.040
In this study,

00:23:36.040 --> 00:23:41.030
you will find that we did
have considerable independence

00:23:41.030 --> 00:23:44.500
of the judiciary but with
the military takeover,

00:23:44.500 --> 00:23:46.713
this independence was also destroyed.

00:23:48.650 --> 00:23:52.560
Our old judges and lawyers wanted us

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:55.860
to re-establish the
independence of the judiciary

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:58.580
and that, of course, is
of primary importance

00:23:58.580 --> 00:24:00.950
because under the military regime,

00:24:00.950 --> 00:24:05.493
the judiciary was totally under
orders from the executive.

00:24:06.430 --> 00:24:09.630
At the trial of political activists,

00:24:09.630 --> 00:24:13.570
the judges quite openly would
wait for a small envelope

00:24:13.570 --> 00:24:15.500
to be put in front of them.

00:24:15.500 --> 00:24:18.090
They would open it and in that envelope

00:24:18.090 --> 00:24:21.310
would be the sentence that
they were expected to pass

00:24:21.310 --> 00:24:25.423
on the prisoner's chart and
they would pass sentence.

00:24:26.310 --> 00:24:29.449
We try to teach the members of our party

00:24:29.449 --> 00:24:34.449
to ask those who came to arrest
them, what the charges were

00:24:34.670 --> 00:24:36.900
and whether they or
not they had a warrant.

00:24:36.900 --> 00:24:39.040
And one of our members just that

00:24:39.040 --> 00:24:43.000
when a troop of security
officers came to arrest him.

00:24:43.000 --> 00:24:46.600
He said, "Do you have a warrant
and what are the charges?"

00:24:46.600 --> 00:24:48.217
And the man said, "Don't be ridiculous.

00:24:48.217 --> 00:24:51.860
"We've already decided what
the sentence is going to be."

00:24:51.860 --> 00:24:54.463
So this is rule of law in Burma.

00:24:55.617 --> 00:24:59.570
And he thought it was perfectly
all right for him to do this

00:24:59.570 --> 00:25:04.050
and he was simply trying
to let our man know

00:25:04.050 --> 00:25:07.440
that he should not engage
in such stupid activities

00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:11.200
as trying to get the law to work for him.

00:25:11.200 --> 00:25:14.290
The law only worked for those in power.

00:25:14.290 --> 00:25:18.810
That was lack of rule of law meant for us.

00:25:18.810 --> 00:25:22.490
The law only worked for
those who are in power,

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:24.550
for those who are in authority.

00:25:24.550 --> 00:25:28.250
It is not there to protect the people

00:25:28.250 --> 00:25:31.790
or to protect our democratic rights.

00:25:31.790 --> 00:25:34.810
But, of course, the military
regime did not believe

00:25:34.810 --> 00:25:36.243
in democratic rights.

00:25:37.360 --> 00:25:42.360
But now, as the chairman of
the committee for rule of law,

00:25:42.640 --> 00:25:45.340
we have to work in a practical way

00:25:45.340 --> 00:25:49.430
to re-establish an independent judiciary

00:25:49.430 --> 00:25:52.330
which has the confidence of the people.

00:25:52.330 --> 00:25:55.100
Our people no longer
believe in the judiciary.

00:25:55.100 --> 00:25:59.000
Democracy requires a balance
between the three branches

00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:02.750
of the executive, the
legislature and the judiciary.

00:26:02.750 --> 00:26:06.570
We have hardly a judicial
branch to speak of

00:26:06.570 --> 00:26:09.930
because it had been so
crushed under the executive

00:26:09.930 --> 00:26:11.223
for so many years.

00:26:12.070 --> 00:26:14.720
Some of our advisors in that seminar

00:26:14.720 --> 00:26:19.260
suggested the best way in
which we could re-establish

00:26:19.260 --> 00:26:23.566
an independent and respectable judiciary

00:26:23.566 --> 00:26:28.566
would be to educate people
who would be judges.

00:26:29.310 --> 00:26:32.620
They said, one of the old lawyer said,

00:26:32.620 --> 00:26:37.260
the reason why we did not
have rule of law in Burma now

00:26:37.260 --> 00:26:40.320
was because we no longer had judges

00:26:40.320 --> 00:26:42.790
who were well-trained in the law

00:26:42.790 --> 00:26:46.798
and well-trained in the
practices of justice.

00:26:46.798 --> 00:26:49.870
This is why, he said,
there is so much corruption

00:26:49.870 --> 00:26:51.460
in the courts of Burma

00:26:51.460 --> 00:26:54.463
and simply by producing educated judges,

00:26:55.390 --> 00:26:58.370
we would be able to remedy the situation.

00:26:58.370 --> 00:27:01.130
I think this is too simplistic.

00:27:01.130 --> 00:27:04.800
It is not that easy to

00:27:06.260 --> 00:27:08.340
change the ways of people

00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:10.653
who have become corrupt over the years.

00:27:11.870 --> 00:27:15.670
Judges who have become
corrupt cannot be changed

00:27:15.670 --> 00:27:20.270
simply by sending them to
special courses at Yale.

00:27:20.270 --> 00:27:22.513
They may in fact learn--

00:27:22.513 --> 00:27:25.610
(attendees laughing)

00:27:25.610 --> 00:27:30.610
So, it's not just a question
of acquiring a good education.

00:27:32.140 --> 00:27:36.310
It's a question of attitudes,

00:27:36.310 --> 00:27:38.010
of mindset.

00:27:38.010 --> 00:27:40.210
Over the years, not just as judges

00:27:40.210 --> 00:27:43.950
but as civil servants in general
have become very corrupt.

00:27:43.950 --> 00:27:46.170
They became corrupt to begin with

00:27:46.170 --> 00:27:50.110
because their salaries were
inadequate and in order to

00:27:51.160 --> 00:27:54.990
make sure that they would be
able to leave decent lives

00:27:54.990 --> 00:27:56.730
and educate their children,

00:27:56.730 --> 00:28:00.690
they started asking for
bribes and getting them.

00:28:00.690 --> 00:28:04.000
Now, people who get
used to that way of life

00:28:04.000 --> 00:28:08.763
cannot go back to the
old clean ways easily.

00:28:09.780 --> 00:28:13.430
A civil servant with a
decent salary in Burma

00:28:13.430 --> 00:28:17.100
in the old days could
live a respectful life.

00:28:17.100 --> 00:28:19.450
He would have a house,
he would have a car.

00:28:19.450 --> 00:28:22.820
His children would be
able to go to good schools

00:28:22.820 --> 00:28:25.930
and he would be respected by
the community in which he lived

00:28:25.930 --> 00:28:29.520
and that was enough
for civil servants then

00:28:29.520 --> 00:28:31.830
but during the years of corruption,

00:28:31.830 --> 00:28:35.730
civil servants, some civil
servants became very wealthy.

00:28:35.730 --> 00:28:37.850
They would have several
houses, several cars,

00:28:37.850 --> 00:28:41.320
their children would enjoy
all kinds of privileges

00:28:41.320 --> 00:28:43.830
and to expect those people

00:28:43.830 --> 00:28:47.820
to go back to the old
ways would be difficult.

00:28:47.820 --> 00:28:51.030
Even if we were to give
them adequate salaries now,

00:28:51.030 --> 00:28:54.120
the kind of salaries that
would allow them to live

00:28:54.120 --> 00:28:57.550
like well respected civil servants

00:28:57.550 --> 00:29:00.810
but not like millionaires,

00:29:00.810 --> 00:29:02.890
they would not be satisfied with it

00:29:02.890 --> 00:29:06.230
because they've got used
to a different way of life.

00:29:06.230 --> 00:29:11.230
So it's not just re-education
in the technical sense.

00:29:11.330 --> 00:29:16.330
It's re-education with regard
to attitudes and mindset,

00:29:18.120 --> 00:29:20.430
how do we persuade these people

00:29:20.430 --> 00:29:23.610
who have got used to a
very opulent way of life

00:29:23.610 --> 00:29:28.610
that they can make do with a
more modest standard of living

00:29:29.230 --> 00:29:31.690
but need clean lives

00:29:31.690 --> 00:29:36.470
that will gain them
the respect of society.

00:29:36.470 --> 00:29:40.800
It's very difficult so their
rule of law comes in again.

00:29:40.800 --> 00:29:42.960
Action will have to be taken.

00:29:42.960 --> 00:29:44.820
It's not enough simply to say

00:29:44.820 --> 00:29:47.330
we're now paying you a decent salary.

00:29:47.330 --> 00:29:49.360
You live within its means.

00:29:49.360 --> 00:29:53.240
They will have to be laws
that will enable else

00:29:53.240 --> 00:29:57.190
to take action against civil servants

00:29:57.190 --> 00:30:01.323
and against judges who are corrupt.

00:30:02.290 --> 00:30:05.960
So rule of law will engender rule of law.

00:30:05.960 --> 00:30:10.960
We have to start by imposing rule of law

00:30:11.230 --> 00:30:13.283
on the judiciary itself.

00:30:14.380 --> 00:30:17.370
That is not going to be an easy task.

00:30:17.370 --> 00:30:21.630
We need help with this judicial reforms.

00:30:21.630 --> 00:30:25.510
Our committee has started by investigating

00:30:26.410 --> 00:30:30.620
three courts of justice just to find out

00:30:30.620 --> 00:30:34.480
what the actual practices
are, what do the judges do,

00:30:34.480 --> 00:30:37.300
how does the government attorney behave

00:30:37.300 --> 00:30:39.530
and what kind of justice

00:30:39.530 --> 00:30:44.530
do those who come for justice expect?

00:30:44.610 --> 00:30:47.230
These practical measures we are taking

00:30:47.230 --> 00:30:50.290
that we may make practical recommendations

00:30:50.290 --> 00:30:52.740
to the various departments concerned.

00:30:52.740 --> 00:30:55.010
The home ministry, the defense ministry,

00:30:55.010 --> 00:30:59.020
the attorney general's office,
the information ministry.

00:30:59.020 --> 00:31:02.780
All of these will have to
be involved in our efforts

00:31:02.780 --> 00:31:05.413
to re-establish rule of law in Burma.

00:31:08.060 --> 00:31:10.370
If we manage to do this,

00:31:10.370 --> 00:31:14.780
this would be a tremendous
breakthrough for our country.

00:31:14.780 --> 00:31:17.800
Once we can say that we have been able

00:31:17.800 --> 00:31:22.220
to re-establish rule
of law, then we can say

00:31:22.220 --> 00:31:27.220
that the process of
democratization has succeeded.

00:31:28.060 --> 00:31:31.490
Until that point, I do
not think we can say

00:31:31.490 --> 00:31:34.560
that the process of
democratization has succeeded.

00:31:34.560 --> 00:31:38.490
It is not enough just to simply make laws

00:31:38.490 --> 00:31:41.100
which we are doing in the legislature.

00:31:41.100 --> 00:31:45.710
We have to make sure
that these laws are just

00:31:45.710 --> 00:31:47.950
and not only just but that these laws

00:31:47.950 --> 00:31:49.670
are implemented in the right way.

00:31:49.670 --> 00:31:53.600
There must be due process
as well as just laws.

00:31:53.600 --> 00:31:57.740
So until we can do that, we
cannot say that democracy

00:31:57.740 --> 00:31:59.850
is on a safe road.

00:31:59.850 --> 00:32:02.380
People ask me, have we reached the point

00:32:02.380 --> 00:32:04.194
when democracy has become,

00:32:04.194 --> 00:32:07.800
the democratization has
become irreversible?

00:32:07.800 --> 00:32:09.400
And I say no.

00:32:09.400 --> 00:32:14.400
No, because to begin with, the
constitution is in the way.

00:32:14.600 --> 00:32:19.600
The constitution allows the
military extraordinary powers.

00:32:21.070 --> 00:32:25.860
And as long as the military
possesses these powers,

00:32:25.860 --> 00:32:30.090
we cannot say that we are on
the true road to democracy

00:32:30.090 --> 00:32:33.310
which is why we have linked these three.

00:32:33.310 --> 00:32:36.450
Rule of law, end to ethnic conflict

00:32:36.450 --> 00:32:39.200
and amendments to the constitutions.

00:32:39.200 --> 00:32:41.100
These are all linked.

00:32:41.100 --> 00:32:45.010
If democracy is to be
restored to our country,

00:32:45.010 --> 00:32:47.250
we must have all three.

00:32:47.250 --> 00:32:50.570
Without one, the other two will fail too.

00:32:50.570 --> 00:32:52.670
We can't have rule of law

00:32:52.670 --> 00:32:55.290
and not have amendments
of the constitution

00:32:55.290 --> 00:32:56.840
and an end to ethnic conflict.

00:32:56.840 --> 00:33:00.030
We cannot achieve the other
two without rule of law.

00:33:00.030 --> 00:33:05.030
So because of these links, we
put the greatest importance

00:33:05.980 --> 00:33:09.070
on the re-establishment of rule of law.

00:33:09.070 --> 00:33:12.490
It will mean on a practical basis

00:33:12.490 --> 00:33:16.730
that our people can be safer, more secure

00:33:16.730 --> 00:33:20.380
within the laws of the land.

00:33:20.380 --> 00:33:23.670
I am sure I do not have
to explain to law students

00:33:23.670 --> 00:33:27.830
that rule of law is not
the same as rule by law

00:33:27.830 --> 00:33:30.100
and that laws alone are not enough.

00:33:30.100 --> 00:33:32.670
They have to be just laws.

00:33:32.670 --> 00:33:33.503
So,

00:33:34.490 --> 00:33:36.030
Burma is now

00:33:37.530 --> 00:33:39.920
at the beginning of a path

00:33:39.920 --> 00:33:42.570
and I keep repeating
that this is going to be

00:33:42.570 --> 00:33:45.630
the most difficult road we have to travel.

00:33:45.630 --> 00:33:48.763
Too many people think that
there has been a breakthrough.

00:33:49.800 --> 00:33:53.300
I don't think we can say
there has been a breakthrough.

00:33:53.300 --> 00:33:56.360
We have been given the opportunity

00:33:56.360 --> 00:33:59.560
to achieve a breakthrough.

00:33:59.560 --> 00:34:04.560
This morning, there is a word,
achieve, used a few times

00:34:04.820 --> 00:34:07.980
and I made the point that
we have yet to achieve.

00:34:07.980 --> 00:34:09.220
We have not yet achieved

00:34:09.220 --> 00:34:12.463
but we have been given the
opportunity to achieve.

00:34:13.340 --> 00:34:18.340
And in this process, we need
help from different directions

00:34:18.590 --> 00:34:21.500
and I would like to take this opportunity

00:34:21.500 --> 00:34:25.030
to ask for your help and support

00:34:25.030 --> 00:34:29.193
with regard to the establishment
of rule of law in Burma.

00:34:30.120 --> 00:34:32.270
We need well-trained lawyers.

00:34:32.270 --> 00:34:34.620
We need well-trained judges.

00:34:34.620 --> 00:34:38.640
We need a good law school at our,

00:34:38.640 --> 00:34:41.680
good law schools in our universities.

00:34:41.680 --> 00:34:45.480
We need contact between the
members of our judiciary

00:34:45.480 --> 00:34:47.640
and the members of the judiciaries

00:34:47.640 --> 00:34:49.903
of practicing democracies.

00:34:51.290 --> 00:34:53.740
There is so much that we need.

00:34:53.740 --> 00:34:58.620
We need broad education
with regard to write

00:34:58.620 --> 00:35:01.450
as well as responsibilities.

00:35:01.450 --> 00:35:04.370
People are very keen on democratic rights

00:35:04.370 --> 00:35:08.140
but they do not always want to
know about responsibilities.

00:35:08.140 --> 00:35:10.300
But if we want rule of law in Burma,

00:35:10.300 --> 00:35:13.900
we must put equal emphasis on both

00:35:13.900 --> 00:35:16.550
rights as well as responsibilities.

00:35:16.550 --> 00:35:19.970
Citizens who expect the
protection of the law

00:35:19.970 --> 00:35:22.623
must themselves respect the law.

00:35:24.030 --> 00:35:26.750
If we are to achieve all these,

00:35:26.750 --> 00:35:29.340
then I think we can be confident

00:35:29.340 --> 00:35:33.083
that Burma will be firmly
on the road to democracy.

00:35:34.040 --> 00:35:37.840
For years, we have suffered
from lack of rule of law

00:35:37.840 --> 00:35:40.350
which means that we can never be sure

00:35:40.350 --> 00:35:42.950
what is going to happen to us next.

00:35:42.950 --> 00:35:45.710
This is a fact in Burma.

00:35:45.710 --> 00:35:48.640
For many years, we would
have to ask every day

00:35:48.640 --> 00:35:51.380
who was arrested the night before

00:35:51.380 --> 00:35:54.410
because arrest were usually
made in the middle of the night

00:35:54.410 --> 00:35:56.650
and we had to find out in the morning

00:35:56.650 --> 00:35:58.370
which of our colleagues were still free

00:35:58.370 --> 00:36:03.370
and which of them had been put
into prison during the night

00:36:03.410 --> 00:36:05.470
or taken away to the police station

00:36:05.470 --> 00:36:08.763
and often, we would never know
where they had been taken.

00:36:09.720 --> 00:36:13.310
1998, we formed the Committee Representing

00:36:13.310 --> 00:36:14.910
the People's Parliament.

00:36:14.910 --> 00:36:18.800
That is to say this
committee was supposed to be

00:36:18.800 --> 00:36:21.640
a committee representing
those who had been elected

00:36:21.640 --> 00:36:25.230
in the 1990 elections which
were ignored by the military

00:36:25.230 --> 00:36:26.893
because we won too well.

00:36:28.330 --> 00:36:30.960
And because we declared

00:36:30.960 --> 00:36:33.820
that we would be forming this committee,

00:36:33.820 --> 00:36:36.460
many, many of our elected representatives

00:36:36.460 --> 00:36:39.230
were taken away into custody.

00:36:39.230 --> 00:36:42.940
They were told that the authorities

00:36:42.940 --> 00:36:47.530
wanted to discuss the convening
of parliament with them

00:36:47.530 --> 00:36:50.250
but in fact, they were simply taken away

00:36:50.250 --> 00:36:55.250
to military cantonments,
to police stations

00:36:56.170 --> 00:36:59.940
and kept there for weeks

00:36:59.940 --> 00:37:02.720
without contact with their families.

00:37:02.720 --> 00:37:05.793
They were not charged under
any section of the law.

00:37:06.690 --> 00:37:09.763
They were just kept there
week after week after week.

00:37:10.740 --> 00:37:12.873
Then they were allowed to come back home.

00:37:14.020 --> 00:37:16.380
I don't quite know why they
allowed them to come back home.

00:37:16.380 --> 00:37:18.720
Every couple of weeks, they
would be allowed to go back home

00:37:18.720 --> 00:37:21.920
and then they would be taken
back again into custody

00:37:21.920 --> 00:37:25.090
and this went on for more than a year

00:37:25.090 --> 00:37:27.860
and this was how it was in Burma.

00:37:27.860 --> 00:37:30.830
If you have anything to do with politics,

00:37:30.830 --> 00:37:32.950
your life is never secured.

00:37:32.950 --> 00:37:36.193
They can take action against
you in any way they pleased.

00:37:37.930 --> 00:37:40.590
My own experience at Depayin.

00:37:40.590 --> 00:37:45.590
Depayin was 2003 when I was
campaigning in Northern Burma

00:37:46.030 --> 00:37:49.960
and as the campaign proceeded,

00:37:49.960 --> 00:37:53.840
the support of the public
became stronger and stronger

00:37:53.840 --> 00:37:55.830
and one evening while I was traveling

00:37:55.830 --> 00:37:59.640
from one place to another,
our car was stopped.

00:37:59.640 --> 00:38:02.860
It was stopped by a monk who said

00:38:02.860 --> 00:38:06.283
that he wanted me to give a speech.

00:38:07.500 --> 00:38:09.950
Dusk was coming on and we had been warned

00:38:09.950 --> 00:38:13.320
that there might be problems
so we said no, it was too late

00:38:13.320 --> 00:38:15.480
and there were no
arrangements for me to speak

00:38:15.480 --> 00:38:19.390
at that particular place
so we would have to go on

00:38:19.390 --> 00:38:24.390
but he kept on trying to
persuade us to stop for a bit

00:38:25.080 --> 00:38:28.120
and then somebody came
running up from the back

00:38:28.120 --> 00:38:33.120
of our motorcade and said that
troops of thugs had arrived

00:38:33.760 --> 00:38:38.393
and they were beating up
our people at the back.

00:38:39.280 --> 00:38:43.060
This was what the Depayin
incident was about.

00:38:43.060 --> 00:38:46.020
Some of those thugs were dressed as monks

00:38:46.020 --> 00:38:47.700
but they were obviously not monks

00:38:47.700 --> 00:38:50.360
because they all have white
handkerchiefs tied to one arm.

00:38:50.360 --> 00:38:53.120
I think this was to
indicate that they belonged

00:38:53.120 --> 00:38:56.623
to the same group and
they had enormous staffs.

00:38:57.770 --> 00:39:00.390
They killed four of our people.

00:39:00.390 --> 00:39:03.500
They just beat them to
death and they also beaten

00:39:03.500 --> 00:39:05.453
the windows of my car.

00:39:07.600 --> 00:39:09.833
But I had very good driver.

00:39:10.904 --> 00:39:13.300
I did not go on because I felt

00:39:13.300 --> 00:39:14.490
that I had to wait for the others

00:39:14.490 --> 00:39:16.720
and I couldn't just leave
them to be beaten up

00:39:16.720 --> 00:39:19.587
then the young man who
was driving my car said,

00:39:19.587 --> 00:39:21.837
"Look, they're putting
barriers up across the road

00:39:21.837 --> 00:39:25.047
"in front of us," and
I saw that and he said,

00:39:25.047 --> 00:39:26.467
"I think they're trying to cut us off

00:39:26.467 --> 00:39:29.147
"from our people who are coming
from the other direction.

00:39:29.147 --> 00:39:31.690
"Shall I drive through the barrier?"

00:39:31.690 --> 00:39:35.340
So I said, yes, drive through
the barrier so we can meet up.

00:39:35.340 --> 00:39:38.631
Well, what did you what
did you expect me to do?

00:39:38.631 --> 00:39:40.980
(attendees laughing)

00:39:40.980 --> 00:39:42.520
It was very exciting.

00:39:42.520 --> 00:39:45.867
I had never been in a car that
leaped over a barrier before.

00:39:47.450 --> 00:39:49.030
But when he got to the other side

00:39:49.030 --> 00:39:51.290
we found that there was nobody there.

00:39:51.290 --> 00:39:55.440
They was just trying to cut us off

00:39:55.440 --> 00:39:59.710
from all the roads around us

00:39:59.710 --> 00:40:01.420
and I don't know what the intentioned was,

00:40:01.420 --> 00:40:03.580
whether it was just to beat all of us up

00:40:03.580 --> 00:40:07.910
or to arrest all of us but
when we got over the barrier,

00:40:07.910 --> 00:40:10.630
we found nothing and we kept
going and going and going

00:40:10.630 --> 00:40:13.960
until we go to the next
time when we were arrested.

00:40:13.960 --> 00:40:17.980
Now, the point is that we were
the ones who were beaten up

00:40:17.980 --> 00:40:21.700
but we were put in prison
as destructive elements.

00:40:21.700 --> 00:40:23.430
So this is a great mystery to us.

00:40:23.430 --> 00:40:27.510
What had we done to
deserve this imprisonment?

00:40:27.510 --> 00:40:29.153
We hadn't raised a finger.

00:40:31.290 --> 00:40:36.290
Yet, that was the reason why I
was placed under house arrest

00:40:38.030 --> 00:40:43.030
for the next six years which
stretched to seven years

00:40:43.110 --> 00:40:45.830
because there was the
episode of the American

00:40:45.830 --> 00:40:47.340
who came swimming to my house

00:40:48.730 --> 00:40:50.980
bringing greetings from
all of you, no doubt.

00:40:52.360 --> 00:40:56.690
So, I was kept under house
arrest for seven years.

00:40:56.690 --> 00:41:01.600
The last year and a half that
I was kept under house arrest

00:41:01.600 --> 00:41:04.532
on the grounds that I had not turn

00:41:04.532 --> 00:41:07.130
the visitor over to the police,

00:41:07.130 --> 00:41:09.290
I had not turned him over to the police

00:41:09.290 --> 00:41:10.480
because I did not believe

00:41:10.480 --> 00:41:12.900
that he would receive justice from them.

00:41:12.900 --> 00:41:16.410
We had never received
justice from the authorities

00:41:16.410 --> 00:41:19.070
and I thought that it
would be immoral of me

00:41:19.070 --> 00:41:22.520
to turn over somebody
to the security forces

00:41:22.520 --> 00:41:25.640
when I knew that he would
not be given justice.

00:41:25.640 --> 00:41:27.740
Mind you, I shouldn't have
worried so much about him

00:41:27.740 --> 00:41:31.230
because since he was not Burmese,
he was let go pretty soon

00:41:31.230 --> 00:41:34.520
but I was, of course, kept
on for another 18 months

00:41:34.520 --> 00:41:37.850
beyond the time when I
should have been released.

00:41:37.850 --> 00:41:42.850
This, I think, was basically
to keep me under house arrest

00:41:43.070 --> 00:41:47.180
while the elections of
2010 were conducted.

00:41:47.180 --> 00:41:50.580
So the law can be used in any way

00:41:50.580 --> 00:41:52.420
that the authorities please

00:41:52.420 --> 00:41:55.020
to promote their own political agenda

00:41:55.020 --> 00:41:57.073
and that is certainly not rule of law.

00:41:58.210 --> 00:42:02.450
Because of that, we are working
very hard in the legislature

00:42:02.450 --> 00:42:06.530
to re-establish rule
of law and to make sure

00:42:06.530 --> 00:42:11.220
that it goes hand in hand
with other democratic reforms.

00:42:11.220 --> 00:42:15.110
If there are no substantial
reforms to the judiciary,

00:42:15.110 --> 00:42:18.400
we cannot believe that
this is a true process

00:42:18.400 --> 00:42:22.290
of democratization and
I hope this is something

00:42:22.290 --> 00:42:24.200
that you will keep in mind,

00:42:24.200 --> 00:42:27.050
that you will watch what
is going on in Burma

00:42:27.050 --> 00:42:30.430
and be aware of the fact that we have,

00:42:30.430 --> 00:42:33.300
that our judiciary at the longest moment

00:42:33.300 --> 00:42:38.300
is practically non-existent
and until we have a strong,

00:42:38.730 --> 00:42:40.863
independent, clean judiciary,

00:42:41.840 --> 00:42:45.863
we cannot say that Burma is
truly on the road to democracy.

00:42:47.260 --> 00:42:49.910
I'm saying this because
people have asked me

00:42:49.910 --> 00:42:54.460
when they can assume that
Burma has reached the point

00:42:54.460 --> 00:42:56.890
and the process is irreversible

00:42:56.890 --> 00:42:59.740
and I always say there is no such thing

00:42:59.740 --> 00:43:02.060
as an irreversible process

00:43:02.060 --> 00:43:06.420
because we were practicing democracy

00:43:06.420 --> 00:43:09.750
when the army took over in 1962

00:43:09.750 --> 00:43:12.860
so there is never a point where we can say

00:43:12.860 --> 00:43:16.140
this is irreversible but
they must come a point

00:43:16.140 --> 00:43:18.410
when everybody recognizes it

00:43:18.410 --> 00:43:22.470
that to reverse the process
will be far more painful

00:43:22.470 --> 00:43:24.422
than to continue along that.

00:43:24.422 --> 00:43:25.650
Thank you.

00:43:25.650 --> 00:43:28.900
(attendees applauding)

00:43:32.569 --> 00:43:35.467
- Can I put your microphone?

00:43:35.467 --> 00:43:38.717
(attendees applauding)

00:43:50.570 --> 00:43:53.770
- Did I keep it within 40 minutes?

00:43:53.770 --> 00:43:55.590
- Thank you so much for those remarks

00:43:55.590 --> 00:43:59.150
and for those reflections
on trying to build

00:43:59.150 --> 00:44:01.630
a rule of law in Burma.

00:44:01.630 --> 00:44:04.100
Now, we've asked people
to submit cards tonight.

00:44:04.100 --> 00:44:06.270
I got dozens of wonderful questions

00:44:06.270 --> 00:44:09.080
and many are very much
pertinent to the subject

00:44:09.080 --> 00:44:10.460
that you were talking about.

00:44:10.460 --> 00:44:15.460
So with I hope excuse me if
I don't read your question.

00:44:16.270 --> 00:44:19.740
There were many duplicated questions

00:44:19.740 --> 00:44:23.470
and I'll do the best I
can to try to synthesize

00:44:23.470 --> 00:44:26.280
the spirit of what came
forward to in the floor.

00:44:26.280 --> 00:44:28.320
But I thought one of the
interesting questions

00:44:28.320 --> 00:44:30.400
about the rule of law, particularly,

00:44:30.400 --> 00:44:33.590
it seems appropriate given

00:44:33.590 --> 00:44:35.540
the difficulty of making this transition

00:44:35.540 --> 00:44:39.010
from a corrupt, essentially lawless,

00:44:39.010 --> 00:44:41.350
well, paper laws but laws

00:44:41.350 --> 00:44:44.863
that are either oppressive
or not enforced.

00:44:46.750 --> 00:44:48.060
How to make that transition

00:44:48.060 --> 00:44:50.427
to the kind of regime you envision?

00:44:50.427 --> 00:44:53.350
Then question is that many
countries around the world

00:44:53.350 --> 00:44:56.730
in the last couple of
decades have experienced

00:44:56.730 --> 00:44:58.240
transitions from having

00:44:59.670 --> 00:45:03.900
oppressive non-democratic
regimes to greater democracy.

00:45:03.900 --> 00:45:07.140
Do you see any examples in
Eastern Europe or Latin America

00:45:07.140 --> 00:45:08.120
or any other parts of the world

00:45:08.120 --> 00:45:13.120
that have gone through a
transition that seem relevant

00:45:13.500 --> 00:45:15.460
or useful to you and thinking about

00:45:15.460 --> 00:45:17.930
how to affect the transition in Burma?

00:45:17.930 --> 00:45:21.290
- Well, I know some examples which tell us

00:45:21.290 --> 00:45:23.220
what we must not do.

00:45:23.220 --> 00:45:28.220
I was speaking to some
politicians from Cambodia recently

00:45:28.420 --> 00:45:33.260
and they remarked that the fact
that we put so much emphasis

00:45:33.260 --> 00:45:35.380
on the rule of law was a good thing.

00:45:35.380 --> 00:45:38.600
They said they had neglected
to do that in Cambodia.

00:45:38.600 --> 00:45:41.370
They had emphasized the
need for free elections

00:45:41.370 --> 00:45:45.240
and a free legislature
but omitted the importance

00:45:45.240 --> 00:45:50.090
of rule of law and they had
said that because of that,

00:45:50.090 --> 00:45:52.830
Cambodia is now having problems

00:45:52.830 --> 00:45:57.670
with taking forward the
process of democratization.

00:45:57.670 --> 00:45:58.650
So this is an,

00:45:58.650 --> 00:46:00.150
if you like a negative lesson,

00:46:00.150 --> 00:46:03.860
we've learned what can happen
without the rule of law.

00:46:03.860 --> 00:46:07.170
But I think in other
transitions such as in Indonesia

00:46:07.170 --> 00:46:12.050
and in South Africa, they
have tried to strengthen

00:46:12.050 --> 00:46:17.050
rule of law as the democratization
process went forward.

00:46:19.420 --> 00:46:24.420
I think in South Africa,
perhaps, they had an easier job

00:46:24.760 --> 00:46:26.870
than we are going have in Burma

00:46:26.870 --> 00:46:29.025
because although there was apartheid

00:46:29.025 --> 00:46:34.025
which was ruled by law
rather than rule of law,

00:46:34.040 --> 00:46:37.900
at least, there were there
were working judiciaries

00:46:38.940 --> 00:46:41.130
which I don't think we
can say off in Burma

00:46:41.130 --> 00:46:43.760
so we'd have to start from scratch.

00:46:43.760 --> 00:46:47.410
We are in a far weaker position

00:46:47.410 --> 00:46:49.240
than many of the countries which have made

00:46:49.240 --> 00:46:52.393
the transition to democracy
when it comes to the judiciary.

00:46:54.720 --> 00:46:57.630
- One interesting question
asks about the judiciary

00:46:57.630 --> 00:47:01.290
and interestingly raises
a number of the things,

00:47:01.290 --> 00:47:04.460
questions you did attempt to address like,

00:47:04.460 --> 00:47:06.270
the question is what
are the major problems

00:47:06.270 --> 00:47:10.700
and how would you, sort
of in a sense, rank them?

00:47:10.700 --> 00:47:13.260
Corruption of judiciary,
the lack of courage

00:47:13.260 --> 00:47:15.560
and enforcing laws against states.

00:47:15.560 --> 00:47:17.263
The competence of judges,

00:47:18.604 --> 00:47:22.080
the respect for the
judiciary by the people

00:47:22.080 --> 00:47:23.700
and the political will for reform

00:47:23.700 --> 00:47:25.716
and it did seem to me you touched almost--

00:47:25.716 --> 00:47:26.549
- All that--
- But, in a way, even more.

00:47:26.549 --> 00:47:29.250
- Plus, of course, the home department.

00:47:29.250 --> 00:47:31.090
That is to say the police.

00:47:31.090 --> 00:47:34.840
We need a clean police.

00:47:34.840 --> 00:47:39.743
We need the people who
enforce these laws to be,

00:47:41.010 --> 00:47:43.530
to able to live within those laws.

00:47:43.530 --> 00:47:46.180
This is why we are cooperating
with the home ministry

00:47:46.180 --> 00:47:50.340
as well as a defense ministry
in trying to re-establish

00:47:50.340 --> 00:47:51.630
rule of law in Burma.

00:47:51.630 --> 00:47:54.490
We cannot just deal with the
attorney general's office,

00:47:54.490 --> 00:47:56.670
I can't just deal with the courts.

00:47:56.670 --> 00:48:00.930
We have to make sure that
a police too are clean

00:48:00.930 --> 00:48:05.750
and capable of carrying out
the process of rule of law.

00:48:05.750 --> 00:48:07.760
- Seems to me it takes
a kind of political will

00:48:07.760 --> 00:48:09.430
on the part, not only populist,

00:48:09.430 --> 00:48:12.222
but of these people whose lives

00:48:12.222 --> 00:48:14.050
were abused to a corrupt regime

00:48:14.050 --> 00:48:15.610
in that transformative ways.

00:48:15.610 --> 00:48:19.270
- Yes, it's very difficult to make people

00:48:19.270 --> 00:48:20.670
uncorrupt as it were.

00:48:20.670 --> 00:48:23.570
As I said, they get used
to certain way of living,

00:48:23.570 --> 00:48:25.680
a certain standard of living

00:48:25.680 --> 00:48:28.560
and it would be difficult to convince them

00:48:28.560 --> 00:48:30.880
that they should take less.

00:48:30.880 --> 00:48:32.070
But on the other hand, of course,

00:48:32.070 --> 00:48:34.433
we can always recruit new members as well.

00:48:35.688 --> 00:48:37.350
- There's several questions here.

00:48:37.350 --> 00:48:41.300
You talked about how the
question of the rights

00:48:41.300 --> 00:48:43.180
of ethnic minorities interweaves

00:48:43.180 --> 00:48:45.633
with the question of
human rights in Burma.

00:48:46.590 --> 00:48:48.315
You wanna comment a
little more depth on that?

00:48:48.315 --> 00:48:52.870
I mean what is the status of
ethnic minorities in Burma now

00:48:52.870 --> 00:48:55.410
and what in the transition
to our democracy,

00:48:55.410 --> 00:49:00.410
how do you see those interests
being properly represented?

00:49:00.560 --> 00:49:03.300
- The great majority of
the ethnic nationalities

00:49:03.300 --> 00:49:07.210
live in the border areas and
as always happens, they can,

00:49:07.210 --> 00:49:09.720
more injustices are committed

00:49:09.720 --> 00:49:13.010
the further away you move
from the center of government.

00:49:13.010 --> 00:49:15.880
This happens everywhere.

00:49:15.880 --> 00:49:19.540
And our people, they are poorer,

00:49:19.540 --> 00:49:22.172
they have less access to justice,

00:49:22.172 --> 00:49:25.560
they have less access to organizations

00:49:25.560 --> 00:49:27.440
that might be able to help them.

00:49:27.440 --> 00:49:32.160
So unless we can make our
ethnic nationalities feel

00:49:32.160 --> 00:49:33.780
that they are on an even footing

00:49:33.780 --> 00:49:35.450
with the majority of Burmese,

00:49:35.450 --> 00:49:39.280
we will never be able to
achieve genuine harmony

00:49:39.280 --> 00:49:40.113
in the country.

00:49:41.590 --> 00:49:44.990
One of the request that
I always make to people

00:49:44.990 --> 00:49:47.530
who want to invest in Burma
whether in development

00:49:47.530 --> 00:49:51.923
or in humanitarian efforts
or in economic enterprises,

00:49:53.360 --> 00:49:57.400
I ask them to try to
work as far as possible

00:49:57.400 --> 00:49:59.490
in the ethnic nationality areas,

00:49:59.490 --> 00:50:03.840
not just to concentrate in that area

00:50:03.840 --> 00:50:08.190
in central part of Burma
where the Burmese live

00:50:08.190 --> 00:50:12.910
but in the outlying areas to
bring up the standard of living

00:50:12.910 --> 00:50:15.570
of our ethnic nationality states

00:50:15.570 --> 00:50:19.070
to that of the central area of Burma,

00:50:19.070 --> 00:50:22.423
and this would help if there were more,

00:50:23.510 --> 00:50:27.520
if our ethnic nationality
areas had more access

00:50:27.520 --> 00:50:29.090
to the wider world.

00:50:29.090 --> 00:50:31.380
There would be fewer
injustice is committed.

00:50:31.380 --> 00:50:33.720
At least they could not
commit this injustices

00:50:33.720 --> 00:50:35.963
and get away with them to that extent.

00:50:36.850 --> 00:50:38.620
- The connection of ethnic minorities

00:50:38.620 --> 00:50:41.630
and religious identity,

00:50:41.630 --> 00:50:45.149
there are many religions
practiced in Burma

00:50:45.149 --> 00:50:47.184
although the majority are Buddhist

00:50:47.184 --> 00:50:48.557
and you obviously, yourself,

00:50:48.557 --> 00:50:50.980
have written about the
importance of Buddhist teachings

00:50:50.980 --> 00:50:53.673
and practice in your own
thinking, in your own life.

00:50:55.970 --> 00:50:58.653
Are there, how would you,

00:50:59.500 --> 00:51:03.220
what role is there for
better understanding

00:51:03.220 --> 00:51:04.700
across the religious boundaries?

00:51:04.700 --> 00:51:08.847
Is that a means to reducing
the inter-ethnic conflicts?

00:51:10.230 --> 00:51:14.690
- Some of our nationalities
practice religions

00:51:14.690 --> 00:51:15.910
other than Buddhism.

00:51:15.910 --> 00:51:18.170
For example, in the Kachin State,

00:51:18.170 --> 00:51:21.690
the Baptist church well as
the Roman Catholic church

00:51:21.690 --> 00:51:24.740
are very strong, particularly
the Baptist church.

00:51:24.740 --> 00:51:27.150
And in the Chin State as well,

00:51:27.150 --> 00:51:30.430
Christianity is the dominant religion.

00:51:30.430 --> 00:51:33.070
And I have to say, with great sadness,

00:51:33.070 --> 00:51:38.040
that there have been
violations against their right

00:51:38.040 --> 00:51:41.803
to worship freely particularly
in the Chin State.

00:51:42.890 --> 00:51:47.890
Chin fans have complained that
pressure has been put on them

00:51:48.200 --> 00:51:51.640
to not to give up their religion

00:51:51.640 --> 00:51:56.640
but to practice their
religion less ostentatiously.

00:51:58.710 --> 00:52:03.710
The Chins, used to, I thought
it was a beautiful custom.

00:52:03.780 --> 00:52:07.800
They used to put up
crucifixes on hilltops.

00:52:07.800 --> 00:52:10.850
I think this is beautiful
because this is their religion,

00:52:10.850 --> 00:52:13.664
they worship it and it's lovely
to see them from a distance

00:52:13.664 --> 00:52:16.730
but at one time, there was a campaign

00:52:16.730 --> 00:52:19.210
to get rid of all these crucifixes.

00:52:19.210 --> 00:52:21.840
A campaign conducted by Buddhist

00:52:21.840 --> 00:52:25.890
and I'm very sorry that this happened.

00:52:25.890 --> 00:52:28.070
I do not think that this kind of action

00:52:28.070 --> 00:52:29.890
should be allowed at all

00:52:29.890 --> 00:52:33.460
and in the Kachin State,

00:52:33.460 --> 00:52:37.750
we have found the Christian
churches do very good work

00:52:37.750 --> 00:52:41.650
with regard to the
internally displaced people

00:52:41.650 --> 00:52:45.360
who have been herded into camps

00:52:45.360 --> 00:52:49.770
because of the conflict that
took place over the last year.

00:52:49.770 --> 00:52:54.110
We can get help to them best
through the Christian churches.

00:52:54.110 --> 00:52:59.110
So a lot of cooperative effort is possible

00:52:59.150 --> 00:53:02.720
and this will not only bring
about better understanding

00:53:02.720 --> 00:53:06.090
between the different religious
groups and ethnic groups.

00:53:06.090 --> 00:53:08.970
I think it is also bring
more practical help

00:53:08.970 --> 00:53:11.010
to our ethnic nationalities.

00:53:11.010 --> 00:53:14.137
- Buddhist monks played a
significant role in 2007

00:53:15.018 --> 00:53:17.450
in the Saffron Revolution.

00:53:17.450 --> 00:53:21.450
Is there any prospect of that

00:53:21.450 --> 00:53:26.450
using clergy and clergymen
to help cross those lines.

00:53:27.007 --> 00:53:28.250
I'd give an example.

00:53:28.250 --> 00:53:30.760
Tony Blair taught a course
here for three years

00:53:30.760 --> 00:53:33.840
on faith and globalization
in which the real focus

00:53:33.840 --> 00:53:37.430
was actually drew from his
experience in Northern Ireland

00:53:37.430 --> 00:53:40.990
where he is getting a peaceful
solution in Northern Ireland

00:53:40.990 --> 00:53:44.070
was in large part, because of his ability

00:53:44.070 --> 00:53:46.460
to work with both Protestant
and Catholic clergy

00:53:46.460 --> 00:53:48.470
to help bridge some of the divide.

00:53:48.470 --> 00:53:50.670
I wonder is that a strategy
that could help with

00:53:50.670 --> 00:53:51.503
the recent ethnic tension?
- I think so.

00:53:51.503 --> 00:53:54.170
I think there are a
number of Buddhist monks

00:53:54.170 --> 00:53:57.540
and Christian clergymen
who are working towards

00:53:58.470 --> 00:54:03.470
such as resolution of conflicts
between their communities.

00:54:03.550 --> 00:54:05.973
- And what about the role of
Muslim populations in Burma?

00:54:05.973 --> 00:54:08.890
- The Muslim populations
are scattered all over Burma

00:54:08.890 --> 00:54:10.030
but, of course, these days

00:54:10.030 --> 00:54:12.200
people hear most about
the Muslim population

00:54:12.200 --> 00:54:15.580
in the Rakhine State because
of the recent troubles

00:54:15.580 --> 00:54:20.580
and there, the communal
problems go back a long way

00:54:20.610 --> 00:54:22.490
and I have to say quite frankly

00:54:22.490 --> 00:54:25.070
that these are not going
to be resolved overnight.

00:54:25.070 --> 00:54:28.440
This is another reason why
I emphasize rule of law

00:54:28.440 --> 00:54:31.340
because rule of law can
help to ease tensions.

00:54:31.340 --> 00:54:34.130
They will not make the
communal problems go away.

00:54:34.130 --> 00:54:37.580
The communal differences cannot
be made to vanish overnight.

00:54:37.580 --> 00:54:41.410
It takes a long time, it takes
understanding on both sides,

00:54:41.410 --> 00:54:45.760
it takes positive action from both sides.

00:54:45.760 --> 00:54:47.260
I'm not saying it's impossible.

00:54:47.260 --> 00:54:52.200
I believe it is possible
because there are Muslims

00:54:52.200 --> 00:54:55.060
in the Rakhine State who have
lived there for generations

00:54:55.060 --> 00:54:57.420
and at peace with their Buddhist neighbors

00:54:57.420 --> 00:55:00.170
so I do not see why this kind of peaceful

00:55:00.170 --> 00:55:03.340
harmonious relationship
should not be re-established

00:55:03.340 --> 00:55:06.320
especially now as the government is saying

00:55:06.320 --> 00:55:09.250
that they will respect
the citizenship laws.

00:55:09.250 --> 00:55:11.440
I think we should also go a step forward

00:55:11.440 --> 00:55:14.184
and review the citizenship
laws to make sure

00:55:14.184 --> 00:55:16.853
that they're in keeping with
international standards.

00:55:18.660 --> 00:55:20.930
Coming back to sort of the
constitutional framework

00:55:20.930 --> 00:55:22.380
which you talked about a bit,

00:55:23.560 --> 00:55:28.160
another question asks
given that the current

00:55:28.160 --> 00:55:31.910
constitution provides for
sort of reserved percentages

00:55:31.910 --> 00:55:34.810
of representation in
parliament for the military,

00:55:34.810 --> 00:55:36.920
do you see that as a hindrance

00:55:36.920 --> 00:55:40.745
to evolution toward rule
of law or democracy?

00:55:40.745 --> 00:55:42.990
- Well, of course, in a genuine democracy,

00:55:42.990 --> 00:55:46.750
you can't have an unelected
portion of the legislature

00:55:46.750 --> 00:55:49.160
which votes on an equal footing

00:55:49.160 --> 00:55:50.930
with those who are elected

00:55:50.930 --> 00:55:53.420
but I have to say that
I really, at the moment,

00:55:53.420 --> 00:55:54.743
don't mind having them,

00:55:55.710 --> 00:55:57.990
the military contingent there

00:55:57.990 --> 00:56:01.494
because that's our best
opportunity to get to know them.

00:56:01.494 --> 00:56:04.320
(attendees laughing)

00:56:04.320 --> 00:56:06.080
It's the truth.

00:56:06.080 --> 00:56:09.130
And I think once they get to know us,

00:56:09.130 --> 00:56:12.430
they will understand that
we are not against them.

00:56:12.430 --> 00:56:14.100
We want to work together with them

00:56:14.100 --> 00:56:18.200
to built up the kind of society
that would be good for them

00:56:18.200 --> 00:56:20.040
as well as for us.

00:56:20.040 --> 00:56:23.290
I've never had so much dealings
with military men in my life

00:56:23.290 --> 00:56:25.013
until I enter the legislature.

00:56:27.130 --> 00:56:31.310
- A couple of questions
that really go to the issue

00:56:31.310 --> 00:56:36.090
of universality versus local variations.

00:56:36.090 --> 00:56:38.850
So one question is about
democratic institutions.

00:56:38.850 --> 00:56:41.130
I mean, do you see democratic principles

00:56:41.130 --> 00:56:43.114
as entirely universal

00:56:43.114 --> 00:56:46.710
or is there room or
would it be appropriate

00:56:46.710 --> 00:56:48.550
to have significant local variation

00:56:48.550 --> 00:56:51.363
and similar question with
respect to human rights.

00:56:51.363 --> 00:56:53.910
Are human rights strictly universal

00:56:53.910 --> 00:56:56.760
or do values and beliefs
in different societies

00:56:56.760 --> 00:56:59.770
were somewhat different
framework for human rights?

00:56:59.770 --> 00:57:02.410
- I believe in the Universal
Declaration of Human Rights

00:57:02.410 --> 00:57:05.200
and I believe human rights
should be universal.

00:57:05.200 --> 00:57:07.170
But with regard to democracy,

00:57:07.170 --> 00:57:09.820
I think there will be local variations.

00:57:09.820 --> 00:57:12.370
There will be regional,
national variations.

00:57:12.370 --> 00:57:15.110
That cannot be avoided
but that does not mean

00:57:15.110 --> 00:57:19.080
I accept their kind of
definition of democracy

00:57:19.080 --> 00:57:21.800
which the military regime adopted.

00:57:21.800 --> 00:57:24.860
I think they called it
disciplined democracy.

00:57:24.860 --> 00:57:26.514
I think that simply means--

00:57:26.514 --> 00:57:27.347
(attendees laughing)

00:57:27.347 --> 00:57:28.835
- That means disciplined democracy.

00:57:28.835 --> 00:57:29.693
- Yes.

00:57:30.660 --> 00:57:32.323
And in fact,

00:57:33.974 --> 00:57:38.530
I'm rather concern that
some people still think

00:57:38.530 --> 00:57:40.820
of the transition towards democracy

00:57:40.820 --> 00:57:44.490
as a transition to its
disciplined democracy.

00:57:44.490 --> 00:57:49.163
That is to say democracy as
acceptable to the military.

00:57:50.240 --> 00:57:53.943
- So one area in the realm of human rights

00:57:53.943 --> 00:57:56.650
in which there is actually
quite a bit of variation

00:57:56.650 --> 00:58:00.760
across democracies is free speech

00:58:00.760 --> 00:58:03.163
we're in this country, for example,

00:58:05.180 --> 00:58:08.700
speech is protected even
if it is blasphemous

00:58:08.700 --> 00:58:12.500
or criticizes another person's religion

00:58:12.500 --> 00:58:15.030
but in many societies,
that kind of speech,

00:58:15.030 --> 00:58:17.280
even though there's general
freedom of expression,

00:58:17.280 --> 00:58:19.543
that kind of speech is not tolerated.

00:58:20.380 --> 00:58:21.650
How do you see that in Burma?

00:58:21.650 --> 00:58:24.010
- Under the Burmese constitution,

00:58:24.010 --> 00:58:27.240
you are not supposed to attack
other's religious beliefs

00:58:27.240 --> 00:58:30.090
and I have to say that

00:58:31.150 --> 00:58:35.070
I think attacking
other's religious beliefs

00:58:36.450 --> 00:58:38.340
is not

00:58:39.866 --> 00:58:42.000
a necessary element

00:58:42.000 --> 00:58:44.490
in the Universal
Declaration of Human Rights.

00:58:44.490 --> 00:58:47.640
You should be free to
practice your own religion

00:58:47.640 --> 00:58:50.040
but surely, you should respect

00:58:50.040 --> 00:58:52.000
other people's religions as well.

00:58:52.000 --> 00:58:56.350
If those religions do not
impinge on your own freedom,

00:58:56.350 --> 00:59:01.230
I would never insult another's religion.

00:59:01.230 --> 00:59:03.770
I would never say anything disrespectful

00:59:03.770 --> 00:59:06.220
of religions that not my own

00:59:06.220 --> 00:59:09.080
because I think that in many ways,

00:59:09.080 --> 00:59:13.520
that's an abuse of my
right to freedom of speech.

00:59:13.520 --> 00:59:17.090
You should not use your
own freedoms to hurt others

00:59:17.090 --> 00:59:21.060
or to restrict others
freedoms or to offend others.

00:59:21.060 --> 00:59:24.234
If you do not like what
somebody else is doing

00:59:24.234 --> 00:59:29.234
because it, in some way,
infringes on your own way of life,

00:59:29.680 --> 00:59:32.000
then you can talk it over.

00:59:32.000 --> 00:59:36.180
Why can't we have reasonable dialogue

00:59:36.180 --> 00:59:38.530
with regard to our differences?

00:59:38.530 --> 00:59:41.300
Why do we need to insult others

00:59:41.300 --> 00:59:46.193
because they do not believe in
the way in which we believe?

00:59:48.080 --> 00:59:51.976
- One more aspect of rule of law has to do

00:59:51.976 --> 00:59:54.570
with what can the external world do

00:59:54.570 --> 00:59:57.900
to help the advance of ruling in Burma

00:59:57.900 --> 00:59:59.720
and there are actually
three different questions

00:59:59.720 --> 01:00:01.370
but all pretty much the same,

01:00:01.370 --> 01:00:02.620
well, pointing the same direction.

01:00:02.620 --> 01:00:04.490
What can the international community to do

01:00:04.490 --> 01:00:07.750
to promote the development
of democracy and rule of law?

01:00:07.750 --> 01:00:09.440
What can the United Nations do

01:00:09.440 --> 01:00:11.280
and what can US foreign policy do?

01:00:11.280 --> 01:00:14.860
So people thinking along similar lines.

01:00:14.860 --> 01:00:18.510
Do you wanna comment on
where can the outside world

01:00:18.510 --> 01:00:19.570
be of help?

01:00:19.570 --> 01:00:21.268
- Well, the international community

01:00:21.268 --> 01:00:25.150
needs to be aware of the
fact that we need rule of law

01:00:25.150 --> 01:00:27.722
if we are really to
progress to its democracy.

01:00:27.722 --> 01:00:31.690
I don't want them to ignore
the needs of our judiciary.

01:00:31.690 --> 01:00:34.140
I think there's too much concentration now

01:00:34.140 --> 01:00:38.450
on the economic side of
changes in my country

01:00:38.450 --> 01:00:39.770
and on the political side

01:00:39.770 --> 01:00:42.710
but it seems mainly on the economic side.

01:00:42.710 --> 01:00:46.750
And we have to be a
little careful of that.

01:00:46.750 --> 01:00:48.380
As I said this morning,

01:00:48.380 --> 01:00:51.170
a development is no
substitute for democracy.

01:00:51.170 --> 01:00:54.960
And economic development
is certainly no substitute

01:00:54.960 --> 01:00:56.530
for a weak judiciary.

01:00:56.530 --> 01:00:58.840
So awareness is the first way

01:00:58.840 --> 01:01:01.680
in which the international
community can help us.

01:01:01.680 --> 01:01:05.110
With regard to the United
Nations and the United States,

01:01:05.110 --> 01:01:09.340
they could help us in practical ways

01:01:09.340 --> 01:01:13.533
with the training of lawyers,
with the training of judges.

01:01:15.290 --> 01:01:18.380
I want to set up internships
to help our committee

01:01:18.380 --> 01:01:20.960
for the Rule of Law and Tranquility.

01:01:20.960 --> 01:01:24.600
So with the establishment
of such internships,

01:01:24.600 --> 01:01:27.720
there are many practical
ways in which they can help,

01:01:27.720 --> 01:01:30.877
one, our committee and
through the committee,

01:01:30.877 --> 01:01:34.740
the re-establishment of
rule of law in our country.

01:01:34.740 --> 01:01:36.980
- Well, one of the things the
president of Yale can't do

01:01:36.980 --> 01:01:38.310
is commit the Yale Law School

01:01:38.310 --> 01:01:40.649
to any particular course of action

01:01:40.649 --> 01:01:43.620
but I will have a
conversation with our dean

01:01:43.620 --> 01:01:45.090
to see if there's anything we can do

01:01:45.090 --> 01:01:46.883
to be helpful on those lines.

01:01:48.570 --> 01:01:49.860
One last political question

01:01:49.860 --> 01:01:52.760
then I'll turn to a couple of
personal questions to close.

01:01:54.130 --> 01:01:57.820
So why did they let you out?

01:01:57.820 --> 01:02:00.700
And why is this transition
toward democracy taking place?

01:02:00.700 --> 01:02:02.600
What was in the minds of the leaders

01:02:02.600 --> 01:02:04.880
that allowed this to happen?

01:02:04.880 --> 01:02:07.870
- Well, we've been
discussing this quite a lot

01:02:07.870 --> 01:02:12.800
and we tend to forget
the 2008 Constitution.

01:02:12.800 --> 01:02:17.375
I think the 2008 Constitution
was aimed a change.

01:02:17.375 --> 01:02:21.250
The constitution assures the military

01:02:21.250 --> 01:02:26.220
an influential position in
the governance of this country

01:02:26.220 --> 01:02:31.070
and since that constitution
was adopted in 2008,

01:02:31.930 --> 01:02:34.150
I think the military regime felt

01:02:34.150 --> 01:02:37.280
that it was time to go forward

01:02:37.280 --> 01:02:39.701
and to institute the kind of changes

01:02:39.701 --> 01:02:43.320
which would bring positive
benefits to the country.

01:02:43.320 --> 01:02:45.530
There was no doubt about
it that our country

01:02:45.530 --> 01:02:47.330
had got poorer and poorer

01:02:47.330 --> 01:02:50.350
under successive military governments

01:02:50.350 --> 01:02:54.050
and that our people were getting
more and more discontented.

01:02:54.050 --> 01:02:57.850
So this is all part of a program

01:02:57.850 --> 01:03:02.850
which I believe was
decided on sometime back

01:03:02.910 --> 01:03:07.910
and 2010, when the elections
were held to turn the military

01:03:09.060 --> 01:03:11.170
government into a civilian one.

01:03:11.170 --> 01:03:16.120
That is to say the military
members of the government

01:03:17.050 --> 01:03:18.420
retired from the military

01:03:18.420 --> 01:03:22.690
and contested the elections of civilians

01:03:22.690 --> 01:03:26.370
and became the members of
this new civilian government.

01:03:26.370 --> 01:03:28.931
I think most of you know that the majority

01:03:28.931 --> 01:03:33.110
of the members of our government
now are ex-military people,

01:03:33.110 --> 01:03:36.770
the same people who were
in government before 2010.

01:03:36.770 --> 01:03:40.730
But among them are those who wish to

01:03:40.730 --> 01:03:43.510
bring about genuine
reform because they know

01:03:43.510 --> 01:03:45.930
that the country cannot go on in this way

01:03:45.930 --> 01:03:49.120
from economic crisis to economic crisis,

01:03:49.120 --> 01:03:52.170
from social crisis to social crisis.

01:03:52.170 --> 01:03:56.310
And also, I think reform tends
to take on its own momentum

01:03:56.310 --> 01:03:58.980
and this is what we have
to take advantage of.

01:03:58.980 --> 01:04:02.830
For whatever reason, reforms
may have been started.

01:04:02.830 --> 01:04:05.480
We have to make sure
that they are continued

01:04:05.480 --> 01:04:08.570
in the right way and that we all help

01:04:08.570 --> 01:04:10.633
to keep it on the right path.

01:04:12.240 --> 01:04:16.727
- So no fewer than eight
or 10 students, I think,

01:04:16.727 --> 01:04:19.810
asked a version of the following question

01:04:19.810 --> 01:04:20.860
that's more personal.

01:04:21.710 --> 01:04:24.430
What kept you going through
those years of house arrest

01:04:24.430 --> 01:04:26.418
and what kept you focused?

01:04:26.418 --> 01:04:29.840
One form of the question was what advice

01:04:29.840 --> 01:04:33.210
would you give yourself now if
you could turn the clock back

01:04:33.210 --> 01:04:37.100
to 1988 about how to endure that hardship

01:04:37.100 --> 01:04:38.240
or another version of the question

01:04:38.240 --> 01:04:39.670
is what advice would you give to others

01:04:39.670 --> 01:04:41.904
who are facing a similar burden?

01:04:41.904 --> 01:04:45.920
- Well, what advice I would give myself

01:04:45.920 --> 01:04:47.780
if I could turn the clock back

01:04:47.780 --> 01:04:50.040
is that I would have listened much more,

01:04:50.040 --> 01:04:52.470
I would have been a better pupil

01:04:52.470 --> 01:04:54.829
of my music teacher because--

01:04:54.829 --> 01:04:56.797
(attendees laughing)

01:04:56.797 --> 01:05:00.810
Really, I really regretted
the hours I wasted

01:05:00.810 --> 01:05:03.100
when I should have been
practicing my scales

01:05:03.100 --> 01:05:05.350
and exercises because I thought

01:05:05.350 --> 01:05:08.589
if I had really studied
music very, very well,

01:05:08.589 --> 01:05:11.100
it would have been a great solace to me

01:05:11.100 --> 01:05:13.250
throughout the years of house arrest.

01:05:13.250 --> 01:05:14.640
There were other things I could do.

01:05:14.640 --> 01:05:17.700
I enjoy reading, I had books,
I listened to the radio

01:05:17.700 --> 01:05:20.640
but I would very much like
to have been able to play

01:05:20.640 --> 01:05:23.213
the piano really well
throughout this year.

01:05:24.890 --> 01:05:28.110
Inner resources, that
is what is important.

01:05:28.110 --> 01:05:30.798
What you need our inner
resources to keep you going

01:05:30.798 --> 01:05:35.798
under all circumstances
and these inner resources

01:05:36.220 --> 01:05:39.560
you acquire throughout your,

01:05:39.560 --> 01:05:40.920
throughout your life.

01:05:40.920 --> 01:05:43.330
Not just during the
years you are at school

01:05:43.330 --> 01:05:48.240
but throughout your life and
I would say to young people,

01:05:48.240 --> 01:05:51.100
try to strengthen yourself internally.

01:05:51.100 --> 01:05:54.150
Don't depend too much on external factors.

01:05:54.150 --> 01:05:57.850
You must be able to live with yourself.

01:05:57.850 --> 01:06:00.270
I never found it difficult to be alone.

01:06:00.270 --> 01:06:01.920
There were so many things I could do well.

01:06:01.920 --> 01:06:04.718
If there was nothing else,
I could always think.

01:06:04.718 --> 01:06:07.950
You have to learn to do that.

01:06:07.950 --> 01:06:11.680
And also, we have a saying in Burmese,

01:06:11.680 --> 01:06:15.763
what is important for you
is not just to be able to,

01:06:18.771 --> 01:06:19.604
what is the word?

01:06:19.604 --> 01:06:24.604
To suffer the hardships of bad fortune

01:06:24.780 --> 01:06:27.330
but you also have to know how to suffer

01:06:27.330 --> 01:06:29.733
the hardships of good fortune.

01:06:30.640 --> 01:06:32.340
Do you understand what that means?

01:06:33.670 --> 01:06:37.040
That means that you must not be shaken

01:06:37.040 --> 01:06:39.540
by external circumstances.

01:06:39.540 --> 01:06:43.320
You must not be tempted away from the path

01:06:43.320 --> 01:06:46.540
that you set yourself by the hope of gain,

01:06:46.540 --> 01:06:48.615
by the hope of comfort,

01:06:48.615 --> 01:06:51.770
that you should not let
yourself be corrupted.

01:06:51.770 --> 01:06:55.380
To be corrupted is to
give in to the hardships

01:06:55.380 --> 01:06:58.340
of good fortune and you cannot resist

01:06:58.340 --> 01:07:00.900
the temptations of good fortune.

01:07:00.900 --> 01:07:05.130
To be unable to stand up to hardship

01:07:05.130 --> 01:07:07.340
is, of course, not to be able to resist

01:07:07.340 --> 01:07:09.530
the hardships of bad fortune.

01:07:09.530 --> 01:07:12.340
So you have to be able to do both.

01:07:12.340 --> 01:07:14.290
You have to be able to detach yourself

01:07:14.290 --> 01:07:16.450
from the immediate circumstances

01:07:16.450 --> 01:07:18.670
and think of the greater picture.

01:07:18.670 --> 01:07:23.010
You must have an idea of the
sort of person you want to be.

01:07:23.010 --> 01:07:24.950
Unless you have such an idea,

01:07:24.950 --> 01:07:29.590
you will not be able to hold
true to any one path in life.

01:07:29.590 --> 01:07:31.890
Every one of us, especially the young,

01:07:31.890 --> 01:07:34.670
must have an idea of the kind of person

01:07:34.670 --> 01:07:36.460
he or she would like to be.

01:07:36.460 --> 01:07:39.950
I was fortunate in that very early on,

01:07:39.950 --> 01:07:43.280
I knew what kind of person I wanted to be.

01:07:43.280 --> 01:07:45.500
This is linked to my parents.

01:07:45.500 --> 01:07:49.960
I knew what kind of person they
would have wished me to be.

01:07:49.960 --> 01:07:52.290
Now, of course, in this day and age,

01:07:52.290 --> 01:07:54.640
people will think it's very old-fashioned

01:07:54.640 --> 01:07:57.625
to want to comply with the
wishes of one's parents

01:07:57.625 --> 01:07:59.853
but sometimes, it's worth a try.

01:08:00.977 --> 01:08:02.852
(attendees laughing)

01:08:02.852 --> 01:08:04.660
- You know, I had another question

01:08:04.660 --> 01:08:05.840
which I think you just answered

01:08:05.840 --> 01:08:08.170
which is what advice would you give

01:08:08.170 --> 01:08:11.990
to our students here today as
they face their opportunities

01:08:11.990 --> 01:08:14.280
to lead and change the world

01:08:14.280 --> 01:08:15.460
and I think you've just done that

01:08:15.460 --> 01:08:18.110
and so I'm gonna say thank
you so much for inspiring us,

01:08:18.110 --> 01:08:19.690
for being here with us.

01:08:19.690 --> 01:08:20.523
(attendees applauding)

